    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. can you expand on the contingency protocols if a shipment gets delayed at a customs checkpoint or a transit hub? like what are the standard operating procedures for when the monitoring equipment shows a spike or the package sits too long?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the event of a delay at a customs checkpoint or transit hub, follow standard operating procedures to minimize the risk of temperature excursions:\n\n* Immediately contact the shipping carrier and customs authority to report the delay and request an update on the shipment's expected arrival time.\n* Activate the shipment's monitoring equipment, if equipped, to track the temperature data and schedule a follow-up reading.\n* If the temperature monitors indicate a minimum of 2 hours of standby time at a temperature outside the range (-20°C to +50°C), report the incident to the regulatory authorities and initiate a re-temperature control plan (e.g., vaccinating the shipment with dry ice) if necessary.\n* In the event of a temperature excursion, follow established procedures to mitigate the incident, which may include:\n  - Re-temperature controlling the vial shipment with dry ice or other approved materials.\n  - Arranging for immediate delivery or next-day re-shipment if storage at a lower temperature is not available.\n  - Instructing the recipient site or end-user on the acceptance criteria and handling instructions for the re-tempered vials.",
